Save as VowelCounter.java, then javac VowelCounter.java and java VowelCounter. This counts the five unaccented English vowels, case-insensitively; it does not count y.

Pass-by-value gives a function a copy, so changing the parameter leaves the caller’s variable alone. Pass-by-reference gives the function an alias to that variable, so modifications affect the caller.
~~~cpp
#include <iostream>

void incrementCopy(int value) { ++value; }
void incrementOriginal(int& value) { ++value; }

int main() {
    int n=5;
    incrementCopy(n);
    std::cout << n << '\n'; // 5
    incrementOriginal(n);
    std::cout << n << '\n'; // 6
}
~~~
A const reference, such as const std::string&, avoids copying a large object while preventing modification. Use a reference when the function should act on the caller’s object; use value when an independent copy is desired.

~~~java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class BalancedBrackets {
    public static boolean isBalanced(String text) {
        Deque<Character> stack=new ArrayDeque<>();
        Map<Character,Character> opening=Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');
        for (char c:text.toCharArray()) {
            if (c=='('||c=='['||c=='{') stack.push(c);
            else if (opening.containsKey(c)) {
                if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop()!=opening.get(c)) return false;
            }
        }
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isBalanced("([]{})")); // true
        System.out.println(isBalanced("([)]"));   // false
    }
}
~~~
Save as BalancedBrackets.java, compile and run with javac BalancedBrackets.java and java BalancedBrackets. Non-bracket characters are ignored. The stack makes runtime and space O(n). Map.of requires Java 9 or later.

```javascript
function factorial(n) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 0) {
    throw new RangeError("n must be a non-negative integer");
  }
  if (n === 0 || n === 1) return 1;
  return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

console.log(factorial(5)); // 120
```

The base cases `0! = 1` and `1! = 1` stop the recursion. Each other call reduces `n` by one, and the results multiply on the way back out. This direct version is easy to read, but a large input creates one stack frame per recursive call. JavaScript engines impose a finite call-stack limit, so sufficiently large `n` throws a `RangeError` (“Maximum call stack size exceeded”). Also, factorial values grow quickly beyond the range of precise JavaScript `Number` integers; use `BigInt` if you need exact larger results.

Here is a C++17 function that flattens a nested vector represented with a recursive variant. A nested value is either an integer or a list of nested values; the recursion handles any depth.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <variant>
#include <vector>

struct Node;
using List = std::vector<Node>;
struct Node : std::variant<int, List> {
    using variant::variant;
};

void flattenInto(const Node& node, std::vector<int>& output) {
    if (const auto* value = std::get_if<int>(&node)) {
        output.push_back(*value);
        return;
    }
    for (const Node& child : std::get<List>(node)) {
        flattenInto(child, output);
    }
}

std::vector<int> flatten(const List& input) {
    std::vector<int> result;
    for (const Node& node : input) flattenInto(node, result);
    return result;
}

int main() {
    List nested{1, List{2, List{3, 4}, 5}, 6};
    for (int n : flatten(nested)) std::cout << n << ' ';
}
```

Compile with `g++ -std=c++17 flatten.cpp -o flatten`. The example prints `1 2 3 4 5 6`. Runtime is O(N) for N total nodes, with recursion depth proportional to the nesting depth.

```java
public class Prefix {
    public static String longestCommonPrefix(String[] words) {
        if (words == null || words.length == 0) return "";
        String prefix = words[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < words.length && !prefix.isEmpty(); i++) {
            while (!words[i].startsWith(prefix)) {
                prefix = prefix.substring(0, prefix.length() - 1);
                if (prefix.isEmpty()) return "";
            }
        }
        return prefix;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(longestCommonPrefix(
            new String[] {"flower", "flow", "flight"})); // fl
    }
}
```

The function starts with the first string as a candidate and shortens it until each subsequent string begins with that candidate. If there are no strings, or no shared prefix, it returns the empty string. Compile with `javac Prefix.java`, then run `java Prefix`.

```java
public class CapitalizeWords {
    public static String capitalizeWords(String sentence) {
        if (sentence == null || sentence.isEmpty()) return sentence;
        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder(sentence.length());
        boolean beginningOfWord = true;

        for (char ch : sentence.toCharArray()) {
            if (Character.isWhitespace(ch)) {
                result.append(ch);
                beginningOfWord = true;
            } else {
                result.append(beginningOfWord ? Character.toUpperCase(ch) : ch);
                beginningOfWord = false;
            }
        }
        return result.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(capitalizeWords("hello, new world!"));
        // Hello, New World!
    }
}
```

This preserves the original spacing and punctuation and uppercases the first non-whitespace character after each whitespace run. It does not lowercase the rest of each word.

```python
def brackets_balanced(text: str) -> bool:
    """Return True if (), [], and {} are properly balanced and nested."""
    opening = "([{"
    matching = {")": "(", "]": "[", "}": "{"}
    }
    stack = []
    for char in text:
        if char in opening:
            stack.append(char)
        elif char in matching:
            if not stack or stack.pop() != matching[char]:
                return False
    return not stack

print(brackets_balanced("([]{})"))  # True
print(brackets_balanced("([)]"))    # False
```

The stack remembers unmatched opening brackets. A closing bracket must match the most recently opened one, which enforces correct nesting. Any non-bracket characters are ignored; if you want to reject them, add an `else` branch returning `False`.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

void permute(std::string& text, std::size_t index) {
    if (index == text.size()) {
        std::cout << text << '\n';
        return;
    }
    for (std::size_t i = index; i < text.size(); ++i) {
        std::swap(text[index], text[i]);
        permute(text, index + 1);
        std::swap(text[index], text[i]); // restore for the next choice
    }
}

int main() {
    std::string text = "cat";
    permute(text, 0);
}
```

Compile with `g++ -std=c++17 permutations.cpp -o permutations`. The program prints all permutations. If the string has repeated characters, this swap-based version can print duplicate permutations; for distinct outputs, sort the string first and use `std::next_permutation` in a loop. There are `n!` permutations, so the output grows rapidly.

```javascript
function isValidEmail(value) {
  if (typeof value !== "string" || value.length > 254) return false;
  // Practical check for ordinary email addresses; full validation requires
  // following the complete email standard and confirming delivery.
  const pattern = /^[A-Za-z0-9.!#$%&'*+/=?^_`{|}~-]+@[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9-]{0,61}[A-Za-z0-9])?(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9-]{0,61}[A-Za-z0-9])?)+$/;
  return pattern.test(value);
}

console.log(isValidEmail("name@example.com")); // true
console.log(isValidEmail("name@localhost"));  // false
```

This checks a common ASCII email form with a domain containing at least one dot. Email syntax has edge cases and a regex cannot establish that an address exists or can receive mail. For account signup, combine reasonable syntax validation with a confirmation email.

Java is **always pass-by-value**. The subtlety is that when you pass an object, the value being copied is a reference to that object. The method can use that copied reference to modify the same object, but reassigning the parameter does not change the caller’s variable.

```java
class Box {
    int value;
    Box(int value) { this.value = value; }
}

public class PassingDemo {
    static void changePrimitive(int x) {
        x = 99; // changes only the local copy
    }

    static void changeObject(Box b) {
        b.value = 99; // mutates the shared Box
    }

    static void reassignObject(Box b) {
        b = new Box(7); // changes only the local copy of the reference
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int number = 3;
        changePrimitive(number);
        System.out.println(number); // 3

        Box box = new Box(3);
        changeObject(box);
        System.out.println(box.value); // 99
        reassignObject(box);
        System.out.println(box.value); // still 99
    }
}
```

In languages with true pass-by-reference, a function can receive an alias for the caller’s variable itself and replace what that variable refers to. Java does not provide that behavior for method parameters. The phrase “objects are passed by reference” is misleading: Java passes the reference **by value**.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <regex>
#include <string>

bool isValidEmail(const std::string& email) {
    static const std::regex pattern(
        R"(^[A-Za-z0-9.!#$%&'*+/=?^_`{|}~-]+@[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9-]{0,61}[A-Za-z0-9])?(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9-]{0,61}[A-Za-z0-9])?)+$)"
    );
    return email.size() <= 254 && std::regex_match(email, pattern);
}

int main() {
    std::cout << std::boolalpha
              << isValidEmail("name@example.com") << '\n'  // true
              << isValidEmail("not-an-email") << '\n';    // false
}
```

Compile with a C++11-or-later compiler, for example `g++ -std=c++17 email.cpp -o email`. This is a practical check for common ASCII addresses, not a complete implementation of every allowed email syntax. A regex also cannot verify that the mailbox exists; use a confirmation email when deliverability matters.

Here is a Go implementation using an adjacency list. It returns a shortest path in an unweighted graph, or `nil` when no path exists.

```go
package main

import "fmt"

func shortestPath(graph map[string][]string, start, goal string) []string {
    queue := []string{start}
    visited := map[string]bool{start: true}
    parent := map[string]string{}

    for head := 0; head < len(queue); head++ {
        current := queue[head]
        if current == goal {
            path := []string{goal}
            for path[len(path)-1] != start {
                path = append(path, parent[path[len(path)-1]])
            }
            for i, j := 0, len(path)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
                path[i], path[j] = path[j], path[i]
            }
            return path
        }
        for _, next := range graph[current] {
            if !visited[next] {
                visited[next] = true
                parent[next] = current
                queue = append(queue, next)
            }
        }
    }
    return nil
}

func main() {
    graph := map[string][]string{
        "A": {"B", "C"}, "B": {"A", "D"},
        "C": {"A", "D"}, "D": {"B", "C", "E"}, "E": {"D"},
    }
    fmt.Println(shortestPath(graph, "A", "E")) // [A B D E] or [A C D E]
}
```

Save as `main.go` and run `go run main.go`. BFS explores vertices in increasing distance from the start, so the first discovered route to the goal has the fewest edges. Its complexity is O(V + E) for a graph with V vertices and E edges.

```go
package main

import (
    "errors"
    "fmt"
)

type Stack[T any] struct {
    items []T
}

func (s *Stack[T]) Push(value T) {
    s.items = append(s.items, value)
}

func (s *Stack[T]) Pop() (T, error) {
    var zero T
    if len(s.items) == 0 {
        return zero, errors.New("pop from empty stack")
    }
    last := len(s.items) - 1
    value := s.items[last]
    s.items = s.items[:last]
    return value, nil
}

func (s *Stack[T]) Peek() (T, error) {
    var zero T
    if len(s.items) == 0 {
        return zero, errors.New("peek at empty stack")
    }
    return s.items[len(s.items)-1], nil
}

func main() {
    var stack Stack[int]
    stack.Push(10)
    stack.Push(20)
    top, _ := stack.Peek()
    fmt.Println("top:", top) // 20
    value, _ := stack.Pop()
    fmt.Println("popped:", value) // 20
}
```

This generic implementation requires Go 1.18 or newer. `Push` adds at the top, while `Pop` removes and returns the top item. Both `Pop` and `Peek` report an error for an empty stack.

```javascript
function bracketsBalanced(text) {
  const pairs = { ")": "(", "]": "[", "}": "{" };
  const opening = new Set(["(", "[", "{"]);
  const stack = [];

  for (const char of text) {
    if (opening.has(char)) {
      stack.push(char);
    } else if (Object.hasOwn(pairs, char)) {
      if (stack.length === 0 || stack.pop() !== pairs[char]) return false;
    }
  }
  return stack.length === 0;
}

console.log(bracketsBalanced("([]{})")); // true
console.log(bracketsBalanced("([)]"));   // false
```

The stack checks both matching types and nesting order. Other characters are ignored. `Object.hasOwn` is available in modern JavaScript; on older runtimes, use `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(pairs, char)`.

```javascript
function firstNonRepeatingChar(text) {
  const counts = new Map();
  for (const char of text) {
    counts.set(char, (counts.get(char) ?? 0) + 1);
  }
  for (const char of text) {
    if (counts.get(char) === 1) return char;
  }
  return null;
}

console.log(firstNonRepeatingChar("swiss")); // "w"
console.log(firstNonRepeatingChar("aabb"));  // null
```

The first pass counts each Unicode code point, and the second returns the earliest one with a count of 1. It runs in O(n) time and uses O(k) space for k distinct characters. It returns `null` if every character repeats.

```javascript
// Usage: node average-column.js data.csv amount
// Supports ordinary CSV fields, quoted fields, and escaped double quotes.
const fs = require("node:fs");

function parseCSVLine(line) {
  const fields = [];
  let field = "";
  let inQuotes = false;
  for (let i = 0; i < line.length; i++) {
    const ch = line[i];
    if (inQuotes) {
      if (ch === '"' && line[i + 1] === '"') {
        field += '"';
        i++;
      } else if (ch === '"') {
        inQuotes = false;
      } else {
        field += ch;
      }
    } else if (ch === '"') {
      inQuotes = true;
    } else if (ch === ",") {
      fields.push(field);
      field = "";
    } else {
      field += ch;
    }
  }
  if (inQuotes) throw new Error("Unclosed quoted field");
  fields.push(field);
  return fields;
}

const [file, columnName] = process.argv.slice(2);
if (!file || !columnName) {
  console.error("Usage: node average-column.js <file.csv> <column-name>");
  process.exit(1);
}
const lines = fs.readFileSync(file, "utf8").split(/\r?\n/).filter(Boolean);
if (lines.length < 2) throw new Error("CSV must include a header and data rows");
const headers = parseCSVLine(lines[0]);
const column = headers.indexOf(columnName);
if (column === -1) throw new Error(`Column not found: ${columnName}`);
let sum = 0;
let count = 0;
for (const line of lines.slice(1)) {
  const fields = parseCSVLine(line);
  const value = Number(fields[column]);
  if (fields[column] !== "" && Number.isFinite(value)) {
    sum += value;
    count++;
  }
}
if (count === 0) throw new Error("Column contains no numeric values");
console.log(sum / count);
```

Save this as `average-column.js` and run `node average-column.js data.csv amount`. It prints the arithmetic mean of non-empty numeric values in the named column; nonnumeric cells are skipped. This line-oriented parser supports quoted commas but not quoted fields containing embedded newlines. For arbitrary CSV files, use a dedicated CSV package such as `csv-parse`.

